{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RE-VERIFIED GREEN WITH THE FRESHNESS GUARD IN PLACE. Run https://github.com/navapbc/rebar/actions/runs/30515632763 on merged commit bb951f6e4, ubuntu-latest (native x86_64). Every step SUCCESS; '4 passed in 6.39s'; teardown clean; the failure-logs step skipped because nothing failed.\nWHY I RE-RAN RATHER THAN REUSING THE EARLIER GREEN: the guard added in change 1026 executes in the SESSION FIXTURE, before any test. If it over-matched a prefix or tripped on a transient project-list read it would block the entire suite — and the previous green run could not have covered code that did not exist yet. A prior pass is not evidence for code added after it.\nREADINESS DATA POINT WORTH KEEPING: 'Jira DC ready after 96 tries' = ~8 minutes at the 5s cadence, against 37 tries (~3 min) on the previous run. Same image, same runner class. That ~2.7x spread across consecutive runs is exactly why the 20-minute default budget is not padding — a 5-minute default would have failed this run while the harness was working correctly. Recorded so nobody 'optimizes' the timeout down using a single fast observation.\nALL FOUR SMOKE TESTS PASS against a real Jira 8.17.1 Server: the REST v2 create/read round trip (asserting round-tripped VALUES and that description returns a plain string, i.e. v2/wiki not v3/ADF), PAT bearer auth with a programmatically minted token, the bogus-token contrast case, and the serverInfo deployment/version assertion. Teardown is index-independent throughout.\nJ5's acceptance evidence is complete."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785388773970780001,"uuid":"0bb6ba6e-608c-42e7-9cf5-95b34cbf8a96"}